{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>And I will establish My covenant with you.</b> The establishing of the covenant was needed for the fruits, that they should not rot, and that the wicked men of the generation should not kill him — this is the language of Rashi. And it has support from Scripture, since it says, ״וַהֲקִימֹתִי אֶת בְּרִיתִי אִתָּךְ וּבָאתָ אֶל הַתֵּבָה״ (Genesis 6:18) — hear from this that this covenant is that he would be able to come into the ark without hindrance. And the rotting of the grain he learns from what is said, ״אִתָּךְ״ — was it with him alone that He established the covenant? Did He not also establish the covenant with his wife and his sons?! Rather, He alluded to the covenant of circumcision, which is already ״אִתְּךָ״ — with you. And this is measure for measure, somewhat: for the removal of the foreskin is the removal of the surplus blood that causes putrefaction, and so too the rotting of grain comes about because of the dross that brings putrefaction and decay. And further: the foreskin is called חֶרְפָּה (disgrace), as it says (Genesis 34:14), ״כִּי חֶרְפָּה ו֒׳ לָתֵת אֶת אֲחוֹתֵנוּ לְאִישׁ אֲשֶׁר לוֹ עָרְלָה״. And famine is also called חֶרְפָּה, as it says (Ezekiel 36:30), ״וְלֹא תִקְחוּ עוֹד חֶרְפַּת רָעָב בַּגּוֹיִם״. And by the removal of the disgrace of the foreskin, the disgrace of famine is removed. And this matter is learned from Joseph: when the Egyptians said to him, ״אָסַפְנוּ תְּבוּאָה וְהִרְקִיבָה״, Joseph commanded them to circumcise themselves, and Pharaoh agreed through his hand."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:29.902056782Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}